--- body ---
## Context

SPEC.md's sitter design paragraph states the admission guards exist so the
plugin behaves as "a quiet supervisor with a minimal footprint" — see ticket
0701, which measured the cost of a full-library MD5 read every 30 seconds and
is the reason `nextSweepDelayMS` backs off to a 10-minute idle cadence once
the library is caught up.

That backoff only checked one condition: `phase === 'waiting' && candidates
=== 0`. It never considered the resource-admission gate's own five refusal
phases (`cpu-busy`, `low-memory`, `low-disk`, `storage-unavailable`,
`resources-unavailable`, all set by `scheduler.js:123` from `host.blocked()`'s
return value). A refused sweep leaves `phase` at one of those five strings,
none of which is `'waiting'`, so the reschedule fell through to the 30-second
active cadence — the same cadence used while the sitter is genuinely working
through a backlog.

Net effect: the sitter re-polled the resource gate (a `/proc/meminfo` read, a
`/proc/loadavg` read, a disk-space check) twenty times in a ten-minute window
where the machine stayed busy, instead of once. This is the opposite of the
"quiet overnight" property SPEC.md states, and it is directly contrary to
ticket 0701's own point.

## Relevant files

- `plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js` — `nextSweepDelayMS` (~line 391), the fix site
- `plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js:123` — where `state.phase` is set to the
  refusal reason
- `tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs` — the existing resource-refusal tests and
  the new one this ticket adds

## Actions

1. `nextSweepDelayMS` treats any of the five `host.blocked()` refusal phases
   the same as `'waiting' && candidates === 0` for cadence purposes: back off
   to `IDLE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`, not `SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`.
2. Correct the stale comment on "the sitter recovers on the next sweep once
   the readings come back" (`tests/sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs`), which had
   asserted the old 30 s reschedule as the intended behavior.

## Test

Red-first: `a resource refusal backs off to the idle cadence, not the active
one` arms a permanent `cpu-busy` refusal (`loadavg` pinned above any plausible
CPU count) and asserts the one armed `setTimeout`'s delay is
`IDLE_SWEEP_INTERVAL_MS`. Verified failing (`30000 !== 600000`) against the
unfixed function before applying the fix, and passing after.

## Verification

- [x] `nextSweepDelayMS` backs off to the idle cadence on all five refusal phases
- [x] The new test reddened against the unfixed function before the fix
- [x] The existing "recovers once readings come back" test still passes
      unmodified in its assertions (only its comment was corrected)
- [x] `make check` passes

## Invariants

- The active 30 s cadence is unchanged for genuine in-progress work
  (`'extracting'`, `'census'`, or `'waiting'` with candidates pending).
- No change to what `host.blocked()` itself measures or how often it's called
  within one sweep — only how long the scheduler waits before trying the next
  one after a refusal.

## Exit criteria

A refused sweep reschedules on the idle cadence, proven by a red-first test;
the existing resource-refusal test suite is unaffected; `make check` passes.
